Polymorphism and dynamic dispatch.

Polymorphism means "many forms". It's the idea that a single piece of code can work with objects of many different types, and each object responds in its own way. It's what turns inheritance from mere code reuse into genuine flexibility, and it rests on one mechanism: dynamic dispatch.

Here's the setup. A variable's declared type can be a superclass, while the actual object is a subclass. So you can declare a variable as a bank account, but assign it a new savings account. Through that variable, you're allowed to call any method a bank account promises. But when you call an overridden method, the object's real type decides which version runs — not the declared type. That decision, made at run time, is dynamic dispatch. So even though the variable is typed as a bank account, calling deposit on it runs the savings account's deposit. The compiler checks that the method exists on the declared type; the JVM picks the actual implementation from the real object. That's the whole trick.

Why is this so powerful? Because dispatch follows the real object, you can write code that names only the general type, and still get each subclass's specific behaviour. Picture an end-of-month routine that takes a list of bank accounts and, for each one, calls apply-monthly. Savings adds interest, checking charges a fee — each in its own way. And notice: that routine knows nothing about savings accounts or checking accounts specifically. Add a brand-new account type tomorrow, override apply-monthly, and this loop handles it without a single change. There's no "if it's this type, else if it's that type" ladder. That quality — open to new types, but closed to modification — is polymorphism's real payoff.

Sometimes you need to go the other way: you're holding a supertype reference, but you need something specific to a subtype. You can test with instanceof and narrow with a cast — and modern Java folds both into pattern matching, binding the variable when the test passes. So you write, if acc is an instance of savings account s, then call s dot add-interest — and inside that block, s is already typed as a savings account, with no separate cast. It's useful, but it's also a sign to pause. A pile of instanceof checks often means a method that should really be an overridden method on each subclass instead. Prefer dispatch, and reach for instanceof only at genuine boundaries.

So here's the mental model to keep. The declared type is what you're allowed to call, and the compiler checks it. The real type is what actually runs, and the JVM chooses it. Program to the general type, let each object supply its own behaviour, and your code stays flexible as the set of types grows. This is the engine underneath abstract classes and interfaces — which are the next two sections.
